---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { contactEmail, links } from '../data/site';
---
<Base title="Living Stories Lab" path="/lab" description="The Living Stories Lab: collaborative sermons, pageants, and councils, developed at St. Gregory of Nyssa and shared with other congregations.">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Living Stories Lab</p>
      <h1>One approach, many forms</h1>
      <p class="lead">Living Stories began with preaching. The same commitments shape everything in the Lab: the whole community interprets together, every age takes part, and everyone steps into the story together.</p>
      <p class="prose">Each practice is developed and tested at St. Gregory of Nyssa Episcopal Church in San Francisco, then shared with other congregations in forms that are simple and adaptable. The Lab isn't a formal organization yet. It's a home for this work as it grows.</p>
      <p class="prose">Living Stories Sermons were the Lab's first practice, and <a href="/how-it-works">they're ready to use now</a>. Two more are taking shape:</p>
      <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <a class="card" href="#pageants">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:.4rem">Coming soon</p>
          <h3>Living Stories Pageants</h3>
          <p>The community chooses the scripture, writes the script, and performs it within the liturgy.</p>
          <span class="more">Read more ↓</span>
        </a>
        <a class="card" href="#councils">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:.4rem">Theses &amp; Thrones in beta</p>
          <h3>Living Stories Councils</h3>
          <p>Theology strategy games where youth argue the questions real believers faced in turbulent times.</p>
          <span class="more">Read more ↓</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band" id="pageants">
    <div class="wrap split" style="align-items:start">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Living Stories Pageants</p>
        <h2>A pageant the congregation writes</h2>
        <p>Most pageants hand out a finished script. In a Living Stories Pageant, the community builds it together over several weeks, and the time spent writing it becomes time spent dwelling in the story.</p>
        <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">Easter 2026</h3>
        <p>We started by setting the resurrection accounts from all four Gospels side by side. Over two Sundays, people of every age cut them apart with scissors, spread the pieces across tables, and argued about how to arrange them. The group settled on five scenes, mostly from Luke 24, with Mary Magdalene's encounter in the garden from John 20. They chose to keep the moment when the apostles dismiss the women's testimony as nonsense.</p>
        <p>Through Lent, the cast revised the script each week. There was no memorization and no auditions, so a five-year-old and an eighty-year-old faced the same low bar. On Easter morning the pageant was performed in the round, in the midst of the congregation, as part of the liturgy.</p>
        <p class="muted small">A guide for other congregations is in the works.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src="/img/easter-pageant.webp" alt="Young people in costume reading their parts in front of the icon wall at St. Gregory of Nyssa during the Easter pageant" width="1000" height="1333" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Easter 2026 at St. Gregory of Nyssa</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap split" style="align-items:start;margin-top:3.5rem" id="moses">
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src="/img/moses-pageant.webp" alt="Two readers seated before the congregation, one wearing a paper crown, reading a scene from the Moses pageant" width="1400" height="1050" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>A scene from <em>Moses, Seen From Behind</em>, All Saints Sunday 2026</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <h3>All Saints 2026: <em>Moses, Seen From Behind</em></h3>
        <p>Through the fall, a group met on Sundays to read the Exodus passages from the lectionary together. They decided to tell Moses' life through the people who stood beside him, in imagined conversations after death: Jochebed and Miriam, Aaron and Pharaoh, Zipporah and Joshua, and finally Gregory of Nyssa in dialogue with Moses' staff, with a narrator weaving the scenes together.</p>
        <p>The group chose to honor a hard tension in the story: Moses married into a Midianite family, and Israel later made war on Midian. Footnotes in the script mark what comes from Scripture, what comes from tradition, and what the group imagined.</p>
        <p>There were no rehearsals. Every part was written to be read cold on the day. On All Saints Sunday the two morning services gathered as one, and the pageant took the place of the readings and the sermon, with hymns between the scenes and the last scene flowing straight into the Lord's Prayer.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="councils">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Living Stories Councils</p>
        <h2>Theology you play</h2>
        <p>Councils are multi-day strategy games for youth. Players join historical factions, prepare arguments on the questions those factions actually faced, and watch real events reshape the board. Each one premiered at FOG Camp, St. Gregory's summer program.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="split" style="align-items:start;margin-top:2.5rem" id="theses-and-thrones">
        <div>
          <img src="/img/tt-logo.webp" alt="Theses and Thrones logo" width="200" style="margin-bottom:1rem" />
          <p class="eyebrow">Council one · Available in beta</p>
          <h2>Theses &amp; Thrones</h2>
          <p class="lead">The English Reformation. Players don't study it; they preach their way through it.</p>
          <p>Four factions (Henricians, Evangelicals, Puritans, and Traditionalists) each have real theological positions, a historical theologian, and a team of preachers to deploy across a map of the British Isles. Over five sessions they argue questions like who has authority over the church and whether worship should be in Latin, and test their arguments before judges to win reinforcements. Each session, a real event such as the Act of Supremacy or the Dissolution of the Monasteries reshapes the board.</p>
          <ul class="check small" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
            <li>Piloted at FOG Camp 2025 with players ages 7 to 17</li>
            <li>Best for middle school through college; 8 players minimum, 12 to 20 ideal</li>
            <li>Sessions run 2 to 4 hours, as a week-long intensive or across several weeks</li>
            <li>Includes faction files, a printable map, and links to order the wooden figures</li>
            <li>Beta access: $200 suggested donation per congregation. Pilot sites that give in-depth feedback may receive an honorarium.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="actions"><a class="btn" href={'mailto:' + contactEmail + '?subject=Theses%20%26%20Thrones%20beta'}>Request beta access</a></div>
        </div>
        <figure class="photo">
          <img src="/img/board.webp" alt="The Theses and Thrones game board, a map of the British Isles" width="554" height="624" loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="split" style="align-items:start;margin-top:4rem;padding-top:3rem;border-top:1px solid var(--line)" id="a-house-divided">
        <figure class="photo">
          <img src="/img/ahd-board.webp" alt="The A House Divided game board, a map of first-century Judea, Samaria, Galilee, Perea, and the Decapolis" width="570" height="855" loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Council two · Coming soon</p>
          <h2>A House Divided</h2>
          <p class="lead">Roman-occupied Judea in the Second Temple period. Four factions, one fragile peace.</p>
          <p>Sadducees, Pharisees, Essenes, and Zealots each hold real convictions about how to stay faithful under empire. Where Theses &amp; Thrones is competitive, A House Divided is cooperative: the factions have to work together to keep Rome's escalating occupation from boiling over, even while they disagree about almost everything.</p>
          <p>The board spans 31 districts across six regions, from Galilee and the Decapolis to Samaria, Perea, Judea, and Jerusalem. Roman centurions spread district by district, and an escalation tracker shows how close the land is to a crackdown.</p>
          <p>Each day opens with a debate over a contested verse that every faction reads differently: the Temple and worship, God's sovereignty and Caesar's taxes, idolatry and resistance, and the hope of a messiah. Across the week, each faction builds something with its hands: a model Temple, a growing scroll, a hidden library sealed in a jar, a banner.</p>
          <ul class="check small" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
            <li>Premiered at FOG Camp 2026, St. Gregory's summer day camp</li>
            <li>Built for a five-day week, with daily faction files, debates, and crafts</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="muted small" style="margin-top:1rem">Want to hear when it's ready? <a href={links.newsletter} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Subscribe to the newsletter.</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
